# Programmatic mappings: aes_

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

ggplot2's reference page for [`aes_()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/aes_.html), which
covers `aes_string()` and `aes_q()` too, has no examples, so this notebook says only what the page
describes and where Giggly stands.

`aes()` captures the names it is given without evaluating them; `aes_()` and `aes_string()` build
the same aesthetic mapping from inputs quoted explicitly, `aes_string()` from strings and `aes_()`
from quoted calls or one-sided formulas, which made them easy to call from functions that build
plots. `aes_q()` is another name for `aes_()`. All three are deprecated: ggplot2 now recommends
`aes()` with the `.data` pronoun, `aes(.data[[x_var]], .data[[y_var]])`. A Giggly mapping is a plain
map, `%{x: :wt, y: :mpg}`, given to `Giggly.Plot.new/2` or to a layer; it names each column by an
atom or a string, so a column name held in a variable goes into it as it is, and no function builds
it. An expression is plain data too, a function and the values it is applied to in each row
(`Giggly.Mapping`), so what `aes_()` quotes a function builds as it would any other value.